### Pre-downloaded Core Context Files
  At startup the broker needs the NGSI-LD core context. Previously it was either found in
  the MongoDB context cache (from a previous run) or downloaded from https://uri.etsi.org.
  If both failed, a built-in hardcoded copy was used as last resort.

  A new step has been added between the MongoDB lookup and the network download:
  loading the core context from a local file on disk. The lookup order is now:

    1. MongoDB context cache (persisted from a previous run)
    2. Local file in -coreContextDir (new)
    3. Download from https://uri.etsi.org
    4. Built-in hardcoded copy (airgapped fallback)

  Each context file has the context URL on the first line, followed by the JSON-LD content.
  The filename is derived from the URL (e.g. ngsi-ld-core-context-v1.8.jsonld).
  Pre-downloaded files for all core context versions (v1.0 through v1.9) are shipped in
  the ldcontexts/ directory and copied to /opt/orion/ldcontexts/ in the Docker image.

  This eliminates startup failures caused by network timeouts when downloading the core
  context, which was particularly problematic in CI environments and cloud deployments.

## WebSockets (WIP - ETSI TC DATA has not yet finalized the WebSocket API)
  - WebSocket support is enabled via -wip ws
  - WS upgrade on /ws endpoint, with per-connection receive loop
  - Supported operations over WebSocket:
    - createEntity (POST /ngsi-ld/v1/entities)
    - createSubscription (POST /ngsi-ld/v1/subscriptions)
    - putEntity (PUT /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/{entityId})
    - patchEntity (PATCH /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/{entityId})
    - patchSubscription (PATCH /ngsi-ld/v1/subscriptions/{subscriptionId})
  - Notifications are sent over the WS connection for subscriptions created via WS
  - Message envelope format (same as MQTT): {"metadata": {...}, "body": {...}}

